{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.HoefsmitRep","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HoefsmitRep","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeAlgebra","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.YoungTableaux","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":27,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_nonScalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_nonScalar","kind":"theorem","line":376,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L376-L384","doc":"**`ρ_{(2,2)}` is non-scalar (sorry-free):** the generator `σ_1` is sent to `S1`, whose top-right\nentry is `1 ≠ 0`, so it is not `s·1` for any scalar `s`.  This is the concrete `(2,2)` witness of the\nnon-degeneracy asserted in general by `hoefsmit_seminormal_rep`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_σ2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_σ2","kind":"theorem","line":371,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L371-L374","doc":"`ρ_{(2,2)} (σ_2) = S0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_σ1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_σ1","kind":"theorem","line":366,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L366-L369","doc":"`ρ_{(2,2)} (σ_1) = S1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_σ0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22_σ0","kind":"theorem","line":361,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L361-L364","doc":"`ρ_{(2,2)} (σ_0) = S0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep22","kind":"def","line":332,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L332-L359","doc":"**The shape `(2,2)` seminormal representation** `ρ_{(2,2)} : H_4(q) →ₐ[ℝ] M_2(ℝ)`, generators\n`![S0, S1, S0]`.  Sorry-free for all `q ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.S1_ne_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.S1_ne_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":304,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L304-L309","doc":"`S1` is non-scalar (sorry-free): its top-right entry is `1 ≠ 0`.  Follows from\n`seminormalBlock_ne_scalar` via `S1_eq_seminormalBlock`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.S1_eq_seminormalBlock","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.S1_eq_seminormalBlock","kind":"theorem","line":294,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L294-L302","doc":"The concrete `(2,1)` seminormal matrix `S1` is exactly the generic block at `a = -1/(q³+q)`\n(sorry-free), tying the hand-verified `2×2` case to the general building block. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalBlock_ne_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalBlock_ne_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":282,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L282-L290","doc":"**The generic block is non-scalar (sorry-free):** `B(a) ≠ s·1` for every scalar `s`, because its\ntop-right entry is `1 ≠ 0` while every scalar matrix `s·1` has off-diagonal entries `0`.  This is the\ncrux of the non-degeneracy that distinguishes the seminormal representation from the trivial `q·1`\nrep, isolated for the `2×2` building block. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalBlock_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalBlock_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":270,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L270-L280","doc":"**Quadratic relation for the generic block (sorry-free, every `q ≠ 0`, every `a`):**\n`B(a)² = (q - q⁻¹)·B(a) + (q·q⁻¹)·1`.  Because the block's trace is `q - q⁻¹` and its determinant is\n`-1` identically, Cayley–Hamilton gives `B(a)² = (q - q⁻¹)·B(a) + 1`; `q ≠ 0` is used only to\nidentify the constant `1 = q·q⁻¹` matching the Hecke normalisation `qi := q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalBlock","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalBlock","kind":"def","line":262,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L262-L268","doc":"The **generic axial-distance seminormal block** `B(a)`: the unique `2×2` matrix with top-right\nentry `1`, trace `q - q⁻¹`, and determinant `-1`, parametrised by its top-left entry `a`.  The\nbottom-left entry `a·(q - q⁻¹) - a² + 1` and bottom-right entry `(q - q⁻¹) - a` are forced by the\ntrace and determinant.  Every size-`2` orbit block of the general Hoefsmit matrices is of this form\n(with `a` an axial-distance function of `q`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep21_σ1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep21_σ1","kind":"theorem","line":239,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L239-L242","doc":"`ρ_{(2,1)} (σ_1) = S1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep21_σ0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep21_σ0","kind":"theorem","line":234,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L234-L237","doc":"`ρ_{(2,1)} (σ_0) = S0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep21","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep21","kind":"def","line":208,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L208-L232","doc":"**The shape `(2,1)` seminormal representation** `ρ_{(2,1)} : H_3(q) →ₐ[ℝ] M_2(ℝ)`.\n\nBuilt through `Hecke.lift` from `![S0, S1]`; the three side conditions are `S0_quadratic`,\n`S1_quadratic` (for `i = 0, 1`), `S0_S1_braid` (the one braid, `i = 0`), and vacuous\nfar-commutation (`n = 2` has no `|i-j| ≥ 2`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.S0_S1_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.S0_S1_braid","kind":"theorem","line":198,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L198-L206","doc":"**Braid relation** (sorry-free): `S0 S1 S0 = S1 S0 S1`.  The genuine `2×2` seminormal braid\nidentity, verified for all `q ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.S1_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.S1_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L188-L196","doc":"**Quadratic relation for `S1`** (sorry-free): `S1² = (q - q⁻¹)·S1 + (q·q⁻¹)·1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.S0_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.S0_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":180,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L180-L186","doc":"**Quadratic relation for `S0`** (sorry-free): `S0² = (q - q⁻¹)·S0 + (q·q⁻¹)·1`.\nThe `q⁻¹` terms cancel symbolically (`ring` treats `q⁻¹` as an atom), so no `q ≠ 0` is needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.S1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.S1","kind":"def","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L168-L172","doc":"`σ_1 ↦` the square-root-free seminormal matrix.  Entries are the axial-distance rational\nfunctions of `q`: diagonal `-1/(q³+q)`, `q³/(q²+1)`; off-diagonal `1`, `(q⁴+q²+1)/(q²+1)²`.  Chosen\nso that the trace is `q - q⁻¹`, the determinant `-1`, and the braid relation with `S0` holds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.S0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.S0","kind":"def","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L165-L166","doc":"`σ_0 ↦ diag(q, -q⁻¹)` — diagonal on the two SYT of shape `(2,1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance_same_col","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance_same_col","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L141-L144","doc":"Cells in the same column have axial distance equal to the (negated) row gap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance_same_row","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance_same_row","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L136-L139","doc":"Cells in the same row have axial distance equal to their column gap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance_antisymm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance_antisymm","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L130-L134","doc":"**Axial distance is antisymmetric.**  Exchanging the two cells negates it — which is\nwhat makes a seminormal pair carry the two parameters `a(d)` and `a(-d)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.axialDistance","kind":"def","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L125-L128","doc":"**Axial distance** between two cells: the difference of their contents.  In a standard tableau,\n`axialDistance (cell of i) (cell of i+1)` is the parameter that determines the `2×2` seminormal\nblock of the generator `σ_i`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.cellContent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.cellContent","kind":"def","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L122-L123","doc":"**Content** of a cell `(i, j)` (row `i`, column `j`): `j - i`, as `ℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoSign_σ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoSign_σ","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L109-L112","doc":"`ρ_sign (σ_i) = -qi`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoSign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoSign","kind":"def","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L99-L107","doc":"**Sign representation** `ρ_sign : H_n(q) →ₐ[K] K`, `σ_i ↦ -qi` (`= -q⁻¹` once `q·qi = 1`).\n\nThe quadratic relation `(-qi)·(-qi) = (q - qi)·(-qi) + (q·qi)·1` is again an identity over any\ncommutative ring (`(q-qi)(-qi) + q·qi = -q·qi + qi² + q·qi = qi²`); **no `q·qi = 1` needed**. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoTriv_σ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoTriv_σ","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L94-L97","doc":"`ρ_triv (σ_i) = q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoTriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.rhoTriv","kind":"def","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/HoefsmitRep.lean#L83-L92","doc":"**Trivial / index representation** `ρ_triv : H_n(q) →ₐ[K] K`, `σ_i ↦ q`.\n\nThe quadratic relation `q·q = (q - qi)·q + (q·qi)·1` is an *identity* over any commutative ring\n(the `q·qi` terms cancel: `(q-qi)q + q·qi = q² - qi·q + q·qi = q²`), so **no `q·qi = 1` hypothesis\nis required**.  Braid and far-commutation are trivial because scalars commute. ","signatureChunk":0}]}